   $q$The writer covers the first two miles in two sentences but uses several sentences for the hill. Why is this an effective pacing choice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It is a mistake, because every part of a story should get equal space.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"equal-pacing-myth"},{"text":"It shows that the first two miles were not really part of the race.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It spends the most time on the hardest and most important part of the race.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It makes the reader unsure about the order in which events happened.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-pacing"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It spends the most time on the hardest and most important part of the race.$q$, $q$Writers slow down for the moments that matter most, and the hill is where Daniela struggles and then succeeds. Stories do not need equal space for every part, so the second choice is a false rule.$q$, 'draft', '487a96b0-9069-540a-ae60-fca433bf38ed')
